If you use ChatGPT, Claude or Gemini to draft blog posts, product descriptions or client emails, two separate questions come up. Do the vendors let you use the text commercially, and does anyone hold copyright in it? The answers are different, and mixing them up is the common mistake. This guide sets out what OpenAI, Anthropic and Google publish about output, and what the US Copyright Office has said about whether AI-generated material can be protected. It is general information, not legal advice, and it covers US copyright only. If a lot rides on the answer, ask a lawyer in your country. A disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, which is one of the vendors covered here, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against primary sources. A few vendor pages, including OpenAI's rest-of-world terms, could not be opened by the reviewer, so some wording below comes from OpenAI's Europe terms and business agreement.

## What the vendors' terms say

**OpenAI.** Its consumer Terms of Use say that, as between you and OpenAI, you own the output, and that OpenAI assigns you whatever rights it has in it, if any. OpenAI's business agreement adds that this applies to the extent permitted by applicable law. OpenAI's ChatGPT help page says that, subject to its content policy and terms, you own the output you create, including the right to reprint, sell and merchandise it, on free or paid plans. The terms add two cautions. Output may not be unique, and other users may receive similar output, and OpenAI's assignment does not extend to anyone else's output. OpenAI's consumer Terms of Use also list representing that output was human-generated when it was not as something you cannot do; that wording is not in its business terms or services agreement. Our reading is that you should not pass AI text off as hand-written where you are making that claim.

**Anthropic.** Anthropic's Transparency Hub says customers retain rights to their inputs and own their outputs. The terms themselves are more conditional: its Consumer Terms say that, subject to your compliance with the terms, Anthropic assigns you its right, title and interest, if any, in outputs, and its Commercial Terms say the customer owns its outputs on similar terms. Anthropic's Commercial Terms, which cover the API and business plans, also include an indemnity for third-party intellectual property claims over paid use of the services. It is subject to exclusions, including for modifications you make to outputs and for combining outputs with other content, so read the current Commercial Terms before relying on it. Anthropic's Consumer Terms for Free, Pro and Max plans do not offer that indemnity.

**Google.** Google's Terms of Service say some services let you generate original content and that Google will not claim ownership of it. That is narrower than an assignment. For the Gemini API, Google's additional terms (also available in an archived December 2024 version) say Google may generate the same or similar content for others, that you are responsible for your use of generated content, and that you should use discretion before relying on it, including code. Terms for Gemini inside other Google products can differ, so check the terms of the product you use.

| | Output ownership, per the vendor | Notable caveat |
| --- | --- | --- |
| OpenAI | You own the output; OpenAI assigns its rights, if any | Output may not be unique; consumer terms bar passing output off as human-generated |
| Anthropic | Anthropic assigns its rights in outputs, if any, subject to compliance | IP indemnity is for paid Commercial Terms use, with exclusions |
| Google | Google will not claim ownership of generated content | Others may get similar content; you answer for your use |

## What copyright law says

A vendor saying you own the output does not create a copyright. Copyright comes from the law, not from a contract. In January 2025 the US Copyright Office published Part 2 of its report on AI, which looks at whether AI outputs can be protected. The report is the Office's analysis and guidance, not a court ruling or binding law. Its conclusions, as stated in the report, include:

- Copyright does not extend to purely AI-generated material, or material where a human has insufficient control over the expressive elements.
- Using AI tools to assist rather than stand in for human creativity does not affect whether the output can be protected.
- Copyright protects the original expression a human author contributes, even where a work also includes AI-generated material.
- Given current technology, prompts alone do not give enough control to make the user the author, and revising and resubmitting a prompt does not change that.
- Human contributions can count, such as creative arrangement or modification of the output, and the question is decided case by case.

In practice, that suggests text you generate and publish unedited may carry no copyright in the US even though the vendor says you own it, which could mean others can copy it, though a court would decide. Our own reading is that text you rewrite, restructure and combine with your own writing is more likely to include human authorship that can be protected, to the extent of your own contribution. The report says that, as of January 2025, the Office had registered hundreds of works that incorporate AI-generated material, covering the human author's part. We have not checked later court decisions or Office updates. Other countries treat this differently, so a US answer may not hold where you or your customers are.

## What stays your responsibility

- **Accuracy.** OpenAI's services agreement makes you solely responsible for all use of outputs and for evaluating their accuracy and appropriateness for your use case, and its consumer terms warn against relying on output as a sole source of truth. Check facts, figures and legal or medical claims.
- **Third-party rights.** Output could resemble someone else's work. The vendors' terms put responsibility for your use on you. Some business plans offer indemnities with exclusions, so check the terms of your own plan.
- **Use rules.** Vendors' content and use policies still apply to what you generate and how you use it.
- **Platform and client rules.** Publishers, marketplaces and clients may have their own rules on AI-generated content, which no vendor's terms can override.

## A sensible checklist

1. Confirm which terms apply to your account, since consumer, business and API terms differ.
2. Edit and add your own material, so there is human authorship to protect.
3. Fact-check before publishing.
4. Keep a record of how you produced the text, in case a client or platform asks.
5. For anything high-stakes, such as books, branding or contracts, ask a lawyer.

Terms of service and the law on AI output are both changing, so recheck the live pages before you rely on any of this.
